var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"#Lexbor.jl","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"This package provides a Julia interface to the lexbor HTML parsing library. Lexbor.jl integrates with AbstractTrees.jl to provide an interface for traversing the HTML tree.\n\nThe package parses HTML and queries it with CSS selectors. It also navigates, inspects, and serializes the resulting DOM. New nodes and parsed fragments can be attached to edit it.","title":"Lexbor.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Usage","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"The package exports its parsing, querying, and node inspection functions. The navigation, serialization, and mutation functions are public but unexported. Prefer qualified identifiers for both.\n\njulia> import Lexbor","title":"Usage"},{"category":"section","location":"#Parsing-HTML","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"Create a new DOM object using the Document constructor.\n\njulia> doc = Lexbor.Document(\"<div class='callout'><a href='#'>Link</a></div>\")\nDocument(source = nothing)\n\nOr you can parse a file with Base.open:\n\njulia> doc = open(Lexbor.Document, \"file.html\")\nDocument(source = \"file.html\")","title":"Parsing HTML"},{"category":"section","location":"#Querying-documents","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"Use query to search for nodes within the document that match the provided CSS selector.\n\njulia> links = Lexbor.query(doc, \"a\")\n1-element Vector{Node}:\n <a>\n\njulia> callouts = Lexbor.query(doc, \"div.callout\")\n1-element Vector{Node}:\n <div>\n\nquery also supports passing a function as the first argument that will be called on each matching Node that is found. Using this method avoids allocating a vector and iterating over the results twice if they don't need to be stored.\n\njulia> Lexbor.query(doc, \"a\") do link\n           @show Lexbor.attributes(link)\n       end\nLexbor.attributes(link) = Dict{String, Union{Nothing, String}}(\"href\" => \"#\")","title":"Querying documents"},{"category":"section","location":"#Iteration","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"You can use any AbstractTrees iterators to traverse the document contents.\n\njulia> import AbstractTrees\njulia> for node in AbstractTrees.PreOrderDFS(Lexbor.Node(doc))\n           if Lexbor.is_text(node)\n               @show Lexbor.text(node)\n           end\n       end\nLexbor.text(node) = \"\\n    \"\nLexbor.text(node) = \"Link\"\nLexbor.text(node) = \"\\n\"\nLexbor.text(node) = \"\\n\"\n\nThis uses is_text to check whether the current node is a plain text Node and then displays the text content of it using text. Note that newlines and other whitespace is preserved by lexbor's parsing.\n\nOther predicates and accessors available are:\n\nis_element\nis_comment\ntag\ncomment","title":"Iteration"},{"category":"section","location":"#Matching-Nodes","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"Matcher allows for testing a Node to determine whether it matches the given CSS selector.\n\njulia> matcher = Lexbor.Matcher(\"div.callout\")\nMatcher(\"div.callout\")\njulia> for node in AbstractTrees.PreOrderDFS(Lexbor.Node(doc))\n           if matcher(node)\n               @show node\n           end\n       end\nnode = <div>\n\nAs with query you can pass a function as the first argument to a Matcher object in which case it will get called when the Node matches and will return nothing instead of true/false.\n\njulia> for node in AbstractTrees.PreOrderDFS(Lexbor.Node(doc))\n           matcher(node) do matched\n               @show matched\n           end\n       end\nmatched = <div>","title":"Matching Nodes"},{"category":"section","location":"#Navigating-nodes","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"Move around the tree relative to a given Node. Each accessor returns a Node or nothing when there is no such node.\n\njulia> div = only(Lexbor.query(doc, \"div.callout\"))\n<div>\njulia> link = only(Lexbor.query(doc, \"a\"))\n<a>\njulia> Lexbor.parent_node(link)\n<div>\njulia> Lexbor.next_sibling(link)\n\"\\n\"\njulia> Lexbor.prev_sibling(link)\n\"\\n    \"\njulia> Lexbor.first_child(div)\n\"\\n    \"\njulia> Lexbor.last_child(div)\n\"\\n\"\n\nThe accessors are parent_node, next_sibling, prev_sibling, first_child, and last_child. They step through every kind of node, including whitespace text and comments. next_element, prev_element, first_element, and last_element skip to the nearest element instead.\n\njulia> spaced = Lexbor.Document(\"<ul> <li>a</li> <!--x--> <li>b</li> </ul>\")\nDocument(source = nothing)\njulia> items = only(Lexbor.query(spaced, \"ul\"))\n<ul>\njulia> Lexbor.first_child(items)\n\" \"\njulia> Lexbor.first_element(items)\n<li>\njulia> Lexbor.next_element(Lexbor.first_element(items))\n<li>\n\nclosest walks up from a node, itself included, to the first element matching a CSS selector.\n\njulia> Lexbor.closest(link, \"div.callout\")\n<div>\njulia> Lexbor.closest(link, \"table\")\n\nNode implements the AbstractTrees parent interface, so generic tree utilities work on it directly: AbstractTrees.getroot, AbstractTrees.isroot, and AbstractTrees.isdescendant. parent_node backs AbstractTrees.parent, and the sibling accessors back nextsibling and prevsibling.","title":"Navigating nodes"},{"category":"section","location":"#Reading-text","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"text_content returns the text of a node and all its descendants, following the DOM textContent property. text reads a single text node and returns nothing for elements.\n\njulia> Lexbor.text_content(div)\n\"\\n    Link\\n\"\njulia> Lexbor.text(div)","title":"Reading text"},{"category":"section","location":"#Attribute-access","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"Read a single attribute of an element with attribute. It returns the value as a String, or nothing when the attribute is absent or present but valueless.\n\njulia> Lexbor.attribute(link, \"href\")\n\"#\"\njulia> Lexbor.attribute(link, \"target\")\n\nhas_attribute tells an absent attribute apart from a valueless one.\n\njulia> Lexbor.has_attribute(link, \"href\")\ntrue\njulia> Lexbor.has_attribute(link, \"target\")\nfalse\n\nUse attributes to read every attribute of an element as a Dict.\n\njulia> Lexbor.attributes(link)\nDict{String, Union{Nothing, String}} with 1 entry:\n  \"href\" => \"#\"","title":"Attribute access"},{"category":"section","location":"#Serializing-documents","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"Serialize a Node or Document back to HTML. outer_html includes the node itself; inner_html emits its descendants only.\n\njulia> Lexbor.outer_html(div)\n\"<div class=\\\"callout\\\">\\n    <a href=\\\"#\\\">Link</a>\\n</div>\"\njulia> Lexbor.inner_html(div)\n\"\\n    <a href=\\\"#\\\">Link</a>\\n\"\njulia> Lexbor.outer_html(doc)\n\"<html><head></head><body><div class=\\\"callout\\\">\\n    <a href=\\\"#\\\">Link</a>\\n</div>\\n</body></html>\"","title":"Serializing documents"},{"category":"section","location":"#Document-accessors","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"Reach the standard parts of a parsed document with title, head, and body.\n\njulia> page = Lexbor.Document(\"<html><head><title>Example</title></head><body><p>Hi</p></body></html>\")\nDocument(source = nothing)\njulia> Lexbor.title(page)\n\"Example\"\njulia> Lexbor.head(page)\n<head>\njulia> Lexbor.body(page)\n<body>\njulia> Lexbor.document_element(page)\n<html>\n\nset_title! changes the title, adding a <title> element when the document has none.\n\njulia> Lexbor.set_title!(page, \"Renamed\")\nDocument(source = nothing)\njulia> Lexbor.title(page)\n\"Renamed\"","title":"Document accessors"},{"category":"section","location":"#Modifying-documents","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"Build up a document by creating nodes and attaching them. create_element, create_text, and create_comment make detached nodes; append_child!, insert_before!, and insert_after! attach them.\n\njulia> mut = Lexbor.Document(\"<div id='content'></div>\")\nDocument(source = nothing)\njulia> container = only(Lexbor.query(mut, \"#content\"))\n<div>\njulia> para = Lexbor.create_element(mut, \"p\")\n<p>\njulia> Lexbor.append_child!(container, para)\n<p>\njulia> Lexbor.append_child!(para, Lexbor.create_text(mut, \"Hello\"))\n\"Hello\"\njulia> Lexbor.set_attribute!(para, \"class\", \"greeting\")\n<p>\njulia> Lexbor.outer_html(container)\n\"<div id=\\\"content\\\"><p class=\\\"greeting\\\">Hello</p></div>\"\n\nset_text! replaces an element's content, remove_attribute! drops an attribute, and remove! unlinks a node from its parent.\n\njulia> Lexbor.set_text!(para, \"Goodbye\")\n<p>\njulia> Lexbor.remove_attribute!(para, \"class\")\n<p>\njulia> Lexbor.outer_html(container)\n\"<div id=\\\"content\\\"><p>Goodbye</p></div>\"\njulia> Lexbor.remove!(para)\n<p>\njulia> Lexbor.outer_html(container)\n\"<div id=\\\"content\\\"></div>\"\n\nreplace_node! puts one node in another's place. set_inner_html! replaces an element's children with parsed HTML.\n\njulia> Lexbor.append_child!(container, para)\n<p>\njulia> Lexbor.replace_node!(para, Lexbor.create_element(mut, \"hr\"))\n<hr>\njulia> Lexbor.set_inner_html!(container, \"<em>new</em> content\")\n<div>\njulia> Lexbor.outer_html(container)\n\"<div id=\\\"content\\\"><em>new</em> content</div>\"","title":"Modifying documents"},{"category":"section","location":"#Copying-nodes","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"clone_node copies a node within its document. import_node copies a node from any document into another, where it can then be inserted. Both copy descendants unless deep = false.\n\njulia> duplicate = Lexbor.clone_node(container)\n<div>\njulia> other = Lexbor.Document(\"<main></main>\")\nDocument(source = nothing)\njulia> moved = Lexbor.import_node(other, container)\n<div>\njulia> Lexbor.append_child!(only(Lexbor.query(other, \"main\")), moved)\n<div>\njulia> Lexbor.outer_html(other)\n\"<html><head></head><body><main><div id=\\\"content\\\"><em>new</em> content</div></main></body></html>\"","title":"Copying nodes"},{"category":"section","location":"#Parsing-fragments","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"fragment parses an HTML string into detached nodes ready to attach with the same insertion functions.\n\njulia> list = Lexbor.Document(\"<ul></ul>\")\nDocument(source = nothing)\njulia> ul = only(Lexbor.query(list, \"ul\"))\n<ul>\njulia> items = Lexbor.fragment(list, \"<li>one</li><li>two</li>\"; context = ul)\n2-element Vector{Node}:\n <li>\n <li>\njulia> foreach(item -> Lexbor.append_child!(ul, item), items)\njulia> Lexbor.outer_html(ul)\n\"<ul><li>one</li><li>two</li></ul>\"\n\nParsing depends on the context element: a <td> is kept under a table row context but reduced to bare text under <body>.\n\njulia> table = Lexbor.Document(\"<table><tr></tr></table>\")\nDocument(source = nothing)\njulia> row = only(Lexbor.query(table, \"tr\"))\n<tr>\njulia> cell = only(Lexbor.fragment(table, \"<td>cell</td>\"; context = row))\n<td>\njulia> Lexbor.outer_html(cell)\n\"<td>cell</td>\"","title":"Parsing fragments"},{"category":"section","location":"#API","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"type","location":"#Lexbor.Document","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"Document(html_str)\nopen(Document, html_file_path)\n\nParse HTML into an in-memory tree representing the DOM. To parse an HTML file use Base.open.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.Document"},{"category":"type","location":"#Lexbor.Matcher","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"Matcher(selector; first = false)\n\nCreate a new Matcher object that can be used to test whether a Node matches the selector or not. Matcher objects are callable and can be used as follows:\n\nfunction find_first_node(root, selector)\n    # Create the `Matcher` object once, then reuse in the loop.\n    matcher = Matcher(selector)\n    for node in AbstractTrees.PreOrderDFS(root)\n        if matcher(node)\n            return node\n        end\n    end\n    return nothing\nend\n\nThe matcher object has two callable methods. The first, shown above, returns true or false depending on whether the selector matches. The other method takes a first argument function ::Node -> Nothing that is called when the selector matches.\n\nThe keyword argument first::Bool has the same behaviour as the first keyword provided by query. See that function's documentation for details.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.Matcher"},{"category":"type","location":"#Lexbor.Node","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"Node(document::Document)\n\nAn iterable object representing a particular node within an HTML Document.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.Node"},{"category":"type","location":"#Lexbor.Tree","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"Tree(document)\nTree(node)\n\nA display type to help visualize DOM structure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.Tree"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.append_child!-Tuple{Node, Node}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"append_child!(parent::Node, child::Node) -> Node\n\nAppend child to parent's children and return child. An already-attached child is moved, not copied. Follows the DOM insertion rules: ArgumentError is thrown when the nodes belong to different documents, when child is parent or one of its ancestors, or when parent is a text or comment node.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.append_child!"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.attribute-Tuple{Node, AbstractString}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"attribute(node::Node, name) -> String | nothing\n\nReturn the value of the name attribute of an element node, or nothing.\n\nnothing is returned both when the attribute is absent and when it is present but valueless (e.g. v-slot:avatar in <template v-slot:avatar>), mirroring how attributes represents valueless attributes with a nothing value. Use has_attribute to tell the two cases apart. nothing is also returned when node is not an element.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.attribute"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.attributes-Tuple{Node}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"attributes(node::Node) -> Dict{String,Union{String,Nothing}} | Nothing\n\nReturn a Dict of all the attributes of a node, or nothing when it is not a valid element node.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.attributes"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.body-Tuple{Document}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"body(document::Document) -> Node | nothing\n\nReturn the document's <body> element, or nothing when it has none.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.body"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.clone_node-Tuple{Node}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"clone_node(node::Node; deep = true) -> Node\n\nReturn a detached copy of node in the same document. With deep = false only the node itself and its attributes are copied, not its descendants. Throws ArgumentError for a document node.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.clone_node"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.closest-Tuple{Node, String}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"closest(node::Node, selector::Union{String,Matcher}) -> Node | nothing\n\nReturn the nearest element, starting with node itself and moving up through its ancestors, that matches selector, or nothing when none does. Pass a Matcher to reuse a compiled selector across calls.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.closest"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.comment-Tuple{Node}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"comment(node) -> String | nothing\n\nReturn the comment content of a node, or nothing when the node is not a valid comment node.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.comment"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.create_comment-Tuple{Document, AbstractString}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"create_comment(document::Document, text::AbstractString) -> Node\n\nCreate a new comment Node containing text, owned by document. The node starts detached; attach it with append_child! or a sibling insertion.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.create_comment"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.create_element-Tuple{Document, Union{AbstractString, Symbol}}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"create_element(document::Document, tag::Union{Symbol,AbstractString}) -> Node\n\nCreate a new element Node with the given tag name, owned by document. The node starts detached; attach it with append_child!, insert_before!, or insert_after!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.create_element"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.create_text-Tuple{Document, AbstractString}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"create_text(document::Document, text::AbstractString) -> Node\n\nCreate a new text Node containing text, owned by document. The node starts detached; attach it with append_child! or a sibling insertion.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.create_text"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.document_element-Tuple{Document}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"document_element(document::Document) -> Node | nothing\n\nReturn the document's root element, normally <html>, or nothing when it has none.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.document_element"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.first_child-Tuple{Node}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"first_child(node::Node) -> Node | nothing\n\nReturn the first child of node, or nothing when it has no children.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.first_child"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.first_element-Tuple{Node}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"first_element(node::Node) -> Node | nothing\n\nReturn the first child of node that is an element, skipping text and comment nodes, or nothing when it has none.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.first_element"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.fragment-Tuple{Document, AbstractString}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"fragment(document::Document, html; context::Node = body(document)) -> Vector{Node}\n\nParse html as a fragment in the context of the context element and return the parsed top-level nodes, owned by document and detached from any tree.\n\nParsing rules depend on context: the same html yields different nodes under different contexts (e.g. <td>x</td> produces a <td> element inside a table context but a bare text node under <body>). The default context is body.\n\nThe returned nodes are ready to attach with append_child!, insert_before!, or insert_after!. context must be an element belonging to document; otherwise ArgumentError is thrown.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.fragment"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.has_attribute-Tuple{Node, AbstractString}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"has_attribute(node::Node, name) -> Bool\n\nReturn whether an element node has the name attribute, regardless of whether it has a value. Returns false when node is not an element.\n\nThis disambiguates attribute, which returns nothing both for an absent attribute and for a present-but-valueless one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.has_attribute"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.head-Tuple{Document}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"head(document::Document) -> Node | nothing\n\nReturn the document's <head> element, or nothing when it has none.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.head"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.import_node-Tuple{Document, Node}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"import_node(document::Document, node::Node; deep = true) -> Node\n\nCopy node into document and return the detached copy, which can then be inserted anywhere in document. node may belong to any document and is left unchanged. With deep = false only the node itself and its attributes are copied, not its descendants. Throws ArgumentError for a document node.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.import_node"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.inner_html-Tuple{Node}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"inner_html(node::Node) -> String\ninner_html(document::Document) -> String\n\nReturn the HTML serialization of the node's descendants only. For a leaf node the returned string is empty; see outer_html to include the node itself.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.inner_html"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.insert_after!-Tuple{Node, Node}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"insert_after!(anchor::Node, node::Node) -> Node\n\nInsert node immediately after anchor among its siblings and return node. An already-attached node is moved, and inserting anchor after itself leaves it in place. Throws ArgumentError when anchor has no parent, and otherwise follows the rules of append_child! for that parent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.insert_after!"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.insert_before!-Tuple{Node, Node}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"insert_before!(anchor::Node, node::Node) -> Node\n\nInsert node immediately before anchor among its siblings and return node. An already-attached node is moved, and inserting anchor before itself leaves it in place. Throws ArgumentError when anchor has no parent, and otherwise follows the rules of append_child! for that parent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.insert_before!"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.is_comment-Tuple{Node}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"is_comment(node::Node) -> Bool\n\nIs node a comment Node? E.g. <!-- ... --> syntax.\n\nUse comment to access the String contents of the comment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.is_comment"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.is_element-Tuple{Node}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"is_element(node::Node) -> Bool\n\nIs the node an HTML element Node? E.g. a <a>, <div>, etc.\n\nUse tag to access the name of the element as a Symbol and use attributes to access the element attributes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.is_element"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.is_text-Tuple{Node}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"is_text(node::Node) -> Bool\n\nIs the node a plain text string?\n\nUse text to access the String contents of the node.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.is_text"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.last_child-Tuple{Node}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"last_child(node::Node) -> Node | nothing\n\nReturn the last child of node, or nothing when it has no children.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.last_child"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.last_element-Tuple{Node}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"last_element(node::Node) -> Node | nothing\n\nReturn the last child of node that is an element, skipping text and comment nodes, or nothing when it has none.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.last_element"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.next_element-Tuple{Node}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"next_element(node::Node) -> Node | nothing\n\nReturn the next sibling of node that is an element, skipping text and comment nodes, or nothing when there is none.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.next_element"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.next_sibling-Tuple{Node}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"next_sibling(node::Node) -> Node | nothing\n\nReturn the next sibling of node, or nothing when it is the last child of its parent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.next_sibling"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.outer_html-Tuple{Node}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"outer_html(node::Node) -> String\nouter_html(document::Document) -> String\n\nReturn the HTML serialization of the node and its descendants. For a text node the returned string is the escaped text; see inner_html for the descendants without the node itself.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.outer_html"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.parent_node-Tuple{Node}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"parent_node(node::Node) -> Node | nothing\n\nReturn the parent of node, or nothing when it has no parent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.parent_node"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.prev_element-Tuple{Node}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"prev_element(node::Node) -> Node | nothing\n\nReturn the previous sibling of node that is an element, skipping text and comment nodes, or nothing when there is none.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.prev_element"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.prev_sibling-Tuple{Node}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"prev_sibling(node::Node) -> Node | nothing\n\nReturn the previous sibling of node, or nothing when it is the first child of its parent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.prev_sibling"},{"category":"function","location":"#Lexbor.query","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"query(document | node, selector; first = false, root = false) -> Node[]\nquery(f, document | node, selector; first = false, root = false) -> nothing\n\nQuery the document or node for the given CSS selector. When f is provided then call f on each match that is found and return nothing from query. When no f is provided then just return a Vector{Node} containing all matches.\n\nThe first::Bool keyword controls whether to only match the first of a selector list. To quote the upstream documentation:\n\nStop searching after the first match with any of the selectors in the list.By default, the callback will be triggered for each selector list. That is, if your node matches different selector lists, it will be returned multiple times in the callback.For example:HTML: <div id=\"ok\"><span>test</span></div>\nSelectors: div, div[id=\"ok\"], div:has(:not(a))The default behavior will cause three callbacks with the same node (div). Because it will be found by every selector in the list.This option allows you to end the element check after the first match on any of the selectors. That is, the callback will be called only once for example above. This way we get rid of duplicates in the search.\n\nThe root::Bool keyword controls whether to include the root node in the search. To quote the upstream documentation:\n\nIncludes the passed (root) node in the search.By default, the root node does not participate in selector searches, only its children.This behavior is logical, if you have found a node and then you want to search for other nodes in it, you don't need to check it again.But there are cases when it is necessary for root node to participate in the search.  That's what this option is for.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.query"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.remove!-Tuple{Node}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"remove!(node::Node) -> Node\n\nUnlink node from its parent and return it. The node stays valid and can be re-inserted; its memory is owned by the Document and freed with it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.remove!"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.remove_attribute!-Tuple{Node, AbstractString}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"remove_attribute!(node::Node, name) -> Node\n\nRemove the name attribute from an element node and return node. Removing an absent attribute is a no-op. Throws ArgumentError when node is not an element.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.remove_attribute!"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.replace_node!-Tuple{Node, Node}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"replace_node!(old::Node, new::Node) -> Node\n\nPut new in the place of old in its parent and return new. old is detached and stays valid, as with remove!. An already-attached new is moved. Replacing a node with itself does nothing. Throws ArgumentError under the same conditions as insert_before!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.replace_node!"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.set_attribute!-Tuple{Node, AbstractString, Union{Nothing, AbstractString}}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"set_attribute!(node::Node, name, value::Union{AbstractString,Nothing}) -> Node\n\nSet the name attribute of an element node to value and return node. An existing attribute is overwritten. Passing nothing sets an empty value (name=\"\"), equivalent to passing an empty string; lexbor cannot store a truly valueless attribute. Throws ArgumentError when node is not an element.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.set_attribute!"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.set_inner_html!-Tuple{Node, AbstractString}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"set_inner_html!(node::Node, html::AbstractString) -> Node\n\nReplace the children of element node with the nodes parsed from html and return node. html is parsed with node as the fragment context, so <td> survives inside a <tr>. The replaced children are detached and stay valid, as with remove!. Throws ArgumentError when node is not an element.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.set_inner_html!"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.set_text!-Tuple{Node, AbstractString}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"set_text!(node::Node, text) -> Node\n\nSet the text content of node to text and return node. On an element this replaces all children with a single text node; the replaced children are detached and stay valid, as with remove!. Markup characters in text are escaped on serialization. On a text node it replaces the text; on a comment node it replaces the comment content.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.set_text!"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.set_title!-Tuple{Document, AbstractString}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"set_title!(document::Document, title::AbstractString) -> Document\n\nSet the text of the document's <title> element, creating the element in <head> when the document has none, and return document.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.set_title!"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.tag-Tuple{Node}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"tag(node) -> Symbol | Nothing\n\nReturn the element tag name, or nothing when it is not an element.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.tag"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.text-Tuple{Node}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"text(node) -> String | Nothing\n\nReturn the text content of a node, or nothing when the node is not a valid text node.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.text"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.text_content-Tuple{Node}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"text_content(node::Node) -> String | nothing\n\nReturn the text of node and all its descendants, concatenated in document order, following the DOM textContent property. Unlike text, this works on elements. Returns nothing for the document node.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.text_content"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.title-Tuple{Document}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"title(document::Document) -> String | nothing\n\nReturn the text of the document's <title> element, or nothing when the document has no <title>.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.title"}]
}
